# Every file `COPY --from=brew /system_files /` puts into this image.
#
# Paths are relative to the payload's own /system_files, which is the same thing
# as being relative to / in the built image. One path per line; `#` starts a
# comment. The Containerfile step immediately before that COPY compares this list
# against what actually arrived and fails the build on any difference.
#
# This is a review record, not a lock file. The `FROM ghcr.io/ublue-os/brew ... AS
# brew` line at the top of the Containerfile pins which payload arrives; this says
# what was in it when a person last looked. Bumping that digest without touching
# this file fails the build, which is the point: the reviewer of a Renovate digest
# bump otherwise sees a 64-hex string and has no way to tell that a new file came
# with it.
#
# Read the file before adding a line here. Everything below is copied into / and
# then signed and published, and the COPY runs after the root-login controls
# earlier in the Containerfile, so an added file wins over them.
#
# Deliberately paths only, no hashes. The tarball's bytes change on every upstream
# release, so hashing would fire on every bump and train people to skip the check.
# New surface is what is worth stopping a build for.

# Login-shell fragments. The Containerfile removes all three: they eval and source
# code out of /var/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew, which brew-setup.service gives to
# UID 1000, and /etc/profile.d and the fish vendor directory are read by root's
# login shells too. See issue #243. They are listed here because they do arrive;
# the removal and the sweep beside it are what prove they left again.
etc/profile.d/brew-bash-completion.sh
etc/profile.d/brew.sh
usr/share/fish/vendor_conf.d/ublue-brew.fish

# PAM resource limits, applied to console and SSH logins. Raises nofile from the
# 1024 default to 4096 for every user and for root. Not a privilege boundary.
etc/security/limits.d/30-brew-limits.conf

# First-boot extraction of the Homebrew prefix, run as root. The Containerfile
# presets it and ships a PrivateTmp=yes drop-in for it, because it stages the
# tarball below through a fixed /tmp path. See issue #258, and the staging check
# after the COPY for the part of it this repository keeps watching.
usr/lib/systemd/system/brew-setup.service

# `brew update` / `brew upgrade` on a timer. Both run User=1000 against the prefix
# that user already owns, so neither is a root execution path.
usr/lib/systemd/system/brew-update.service
usr/lib/systemd/system/brew-update.timer
usr/lib/systemd/system/brew-upgrade.service
usr/lib/systemd/system/brew-upgrade.timer

# `enable` lines for the three units above. The Containerfile presets those three
# by name rather than relying on this file, so a unit added here alone would not
# be enabled -- but a preset file is worth seeing change.
usr/lib/systemd/system-preset/01-homebrew.preset

# The 154MB Homebrew prefix itself, extracted at first boot by brew-setup.service.
# The largest thing this image takes from the payload and the reason the payload
# exists.
usr/share/homebrew.tar.zst
